{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/267916-the-social-enginnering-embed","id":267916,"title":"The Social Enginnering Embed","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Demon: The Descent"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-09-28T20:40:33-07:00","id":267916,"title":"The Social Enginnering Embed","content":"The Social Engineering Embed (FoH pg 107) has always bother me.<br /> <br /> It triggers a Compromise roll when used over the phone or online, which seems counter-intuitive. Phishing e-mails (E.g. &quot;Dear employees, Click the link to update your computer, Ed in I.T.&quot;) and targeted calls (&quot;hi, this is Janet in billing, can you believe it I locked myself out again.&quot;) are common tools for crackers and modern day spies. One of the benefits of this approach is anonymous action, and it seems odd that an Embed based on this would risk the attention of the G-M like an Exploit or Embed like Voice of the Machine might. Embed's aren't supposed to trigger compromise unless they're mucking around with the internal workings of G-M.<br /> <br /> Worse, it makes players less likely to purchase or use this Embed; we've had any number of conversations about Compromise-adverse players, so any Embed that risks compromise is going to be passed over. <br /> <br /> Second, against an average npc with 2 Composure, the Embed needs 4+ successes (1-2 success triggers a the minimum, 3 successes - 2 resolve gets one left of success, same as the minimum)to get more than a single piece of information <i>unless</i> the Gm rules that the target in question just isn't important enough to merit applying resistance.<br /> <br /> Finally, the rewards are small given the detriments and risks: a piece of information and a +1 on a roll at a later time. Compare that to something like Authorized, Data Retrieval, Password Entropy or Open Sesame, that allow huge benefits in terms of access or information, but without a the risk of compromise. <br /> <br /> I might be off base... but I'm just having trouble wrapping my head around this one. Does anyone have any experience using the Social Dynamics in a game? Can anyone sell me on it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2233-agentwestmer","name":"Agentwestmer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2233_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-29T01:00:07-07:00","id":268067,"title":"","content":"I'd imagine it's simple - accessing the phone network, or internet are vast pieces of infrastructure (human infrastructure, not the Capital I GM Infrastracture) that the God Machine would easily have its tendrils all through. Especially as you're short-circuiting the more difficult (but compromise-free) version of simply doing actual phishing or social engineering. The Social Engineering embed is also nice in that it doesn't require you to be in physical proximity of the target - Authorised, Open Sesame, Data Retrieval and Password Entropy require that you need to be in the actual location of the object/person/data container to muck around with it or have some form of unfettered access (like having already broken into and hacked the computer network for Password Entropy) - you can use Social Engineering at a distance and that IS the draw. You don't need to infiltrate a (presumably hostile) installation to get information and as a drawback, it's much more likely to risk your cover. Finally, it's one of the few Vocal embeds that allow you to do things like this - Most of the other tools that let you gain easy access to restricted areas or information are Mundane or Instrumental based.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Embed's aren't supposed to trigger compromise unless they're mucking around with the internal workings of G-M. </div> </div> </div>That's not strictly true.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sidebar, Pg 124, Demon The Descent</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Embeds do not usually risk Cover; they are not overt supernatural manifestations of power. However, some Embeds, especially those that tap into the God-Machine\u2019s workings on a direct level (such as Voice of the Machine) might force a roll to avoid breaking Cover. Dramatic failures often do too.</div> </div> </div> </div>Basically, they don't trigger compromises unless they're overtly supernatural, they tap directly into the God Machine or they're strange enough to be outside of expectations of what 'should' happen. Receiving a phonecall and suddenly spilling sensitive or closely guarded information without the instigator working or manipulating the target is extremely strange. Not to mention, this compromise is fairly benign - you get to roll Cover with a +1 bonus.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1462-xenesis","name":"Xenesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1462_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"410"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}